   "l": "The highest of all:\nthose below know that he is there.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The best of all: the people below know only that he is there.",
   "n": "Nobody is named in this chapter. Every line is about a ruler, and the word 'ruler' never appears — Chinese drops a subject the moment it can be guessed, and here it is guessable from the first two characters onward. A real crux in the second line: our edition reads 下知有之, 'those below know that he is there'. Many later editions read 不知有之, 'they do not know that he is there' — the best ruler being the one nobody notices at all, which is the version most often quoted. The two graphs are easy to confuse in old hands, and the two readings make quite different chapters. Here the oldest witnesses back our edition: both Mawangdui manuscripts and the Guodian slips read 下.",
   "p": true
