   "l": "Good faith not enough therein,\nand there is a want of good faith therein.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Where good faith falls short, good faith is wanting.",
   "n": "焉 here is not the rhythmic cry it was in chapter 15's 豫焉: it is a fused word, 於 'in' plus 之 'it', so it closes the clause and carries a 'therein' at the same time. Same character, two entirely different jobs. And 信 is one word for two things English keeps apart — the trust one is given and the trustworthiness one has. The line does not say which is short, because in Chinese it does not have to; that they are the same word is the point. These two lines come back word for word in chapter 23."
  },
