   "l": "Far off! — he holds words precious.\nThe work completed, affairs brought through,\nthe hundred clans all say: we — so of ourselves.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Remote, and sparing of words: the work finished, the business seen through, and the common people all say, 'We are so of ourselves.'",
   "n": "貴言 is 'to hold words precious', which in practice means to spend few of them; the same thought as chapter 2's 不言之教, 'the teaching that is not spoken'. 自然 is one of the book's fixed terms and our English for it is fixed too: so of itself. It is two characters, 'self' and 'so' — the way a thing goes when nothing is being done to it. The modern Chinese word for 'nature' is this same pair, and that is a later meaning; do not read it back in. 我自然 has no verb at all: 'we — of ourselves', and the people are claiming the work as their own doing, which is exactly what the ruler wanted."
  }
